things a little differently today and  show you the presentation I made to the  long vest gathering in Montreal I called  it the age of the polyglot similar to a  presentation or at least a video I made  here not too long ago and I wanted to  point out that I think we have always  been polyglots if we look at  hunter-gatherer societies in the world  today in the Brazilian jungle or in the  Papua New Guinea there's like literally  hundreds of languages obviously those  people have to be polyglots to  communicate with their neighbors I think  it was always that way I like history  and if we look at the ancient Persian  Empire for example there were so many  languages coexisting there obviously  people were learning and speaking each  other's languages similarly in China as  China gradually expanded they expanded  and spread the Chinese language and they  had to be able to communicate with  people who spoke other languages as was  the case in the Roman Empire it often  meant that the dominant power had their  language sort of superimposed on another  language so initially people were  speaking various languages and then they  learned a new language or developed a  new language which included the language  of the dominant power the same was true  in the Byzantine Empire lots of  polyglots particularly at the government  level i happen to be interested in you  know ukraine and russia and so there you  have people intermingling with the  steppe people communicating with  constantinople eventually it leads to  lithuania conquering much of what became  ukraine yet in the at the same time they  became speakers of a sort of a mixture  of ukrainian and and belarusian until  they formed their union with the poles  but you know a holy roman empire where  they spoke german and various Slavic  languages  the Persian Empire which extended into  the Caucasus the Ottoman Empire of  course which had people speaking very  many different languages people were  mixing the austro-hungarian Hungarian  and of course the leaders like Maria  there's a others folk of rowdy languages  polyglots have been around for a long  time and the most popular languages that  people learn today seem to be the  European languages around the world  because of colonialism these European  languages or Arabic in the case of the  Arab conquests or China because of their  expansion within China a small number of  languages have become dominant  but many languages subsist and I  remember when I moved to Canada in 1951  on a steamship and that's how times have  changed my mother tried to teach me  English and she had a picture book with  lots of pictures and the only thing I  remember and I still remember is the  word for petunia I learned French in  school in school systems in Montreal  didn't learn much here's a quote from a  TEFL website where they claim that the  lack of qualified English language  instructors is one of the largest  challenges to educators and citizens  across the globe which is a dramatic  overstatement of the importance of  English and the importance of qualified  instructors people learn languages  mostly on their own people become  polyglots on their own I learned four  languages of Asia starting with Chinese  back in the day of the open real Cosette  in Hong Kong I learned Mandarin Chinese  I learned Japanese and focused on some  favourite content such as this NHK  series on the history of the Showa era  and of course I have other many many  other materials which enable me to learn  languages on my own without these  qualified instructors that the British  Council is so concerned about  and I learned languages other than  English of course because I'm a native  speaker of English and today of course  there is so much opportunity to learn  other languages Netflix is  super-powerful our browser extension  which enables us to create lessons out  of Netflix movies I'm enjoying it for  Turkish but there are audiobooks there's  so much out there that we don't need  that quote qualified instructor  although there's nothing wrong with  having them or her it's not necessary we  can learn on our own we can google for  grammar the traditional language learner  very often is a studying only English in  certainly in the English air in the  French school system in Canada they  learn English and the English school  system that in French in Japan they  learned English it seems to be one  language and everybody's worried about  making mistakes and in reality we are in  the age of the polyglot where he  explored languages for our own reasons  we connect with other people who are  interested in languages such as at the  launch fest in Montreal the goal is in  many cases to discover the language to  communicate in the language to explore  the language but not necessarily be  perfect in the language to engage to  enjoy and once you become a polyglot and  have a number of languages you can't  speak them all the time you may look get  stronger in one for a while and then  fall back you need to have a lot of  input because you can't be talking to  twenty different language speakers all  the time you've got to be a bit of a  dilettante and the old myths about you  have to travel to the country is not  true and getting a girlfriend or a  boyfriend is not necessarily true  because once you've got 20 languages it  can make you pretty busy if you try to  get a girlfriend or a boyfriend in every  one of the languages that you speak so  that's kind of a quick run-through of  what I presented in Montreal
 

